@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.456 = private unnamed_addr constant [339 x i8] c"Enforce a requirement on the version of Ruff, to enforce at runtime. If the version of Ruff does not meet the requirement, Ruff will exit with an error.\0A\0AUseful for unifying results across many environments, e.g., with a `pyproject.toml` file.\0A\0AAccepts a [PEP 440](https://peps.python.org/pep-0440/) specifier, like `==0.3.1` or `>=0.3.1`.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.457 = private unnamed_addr constant [114 x i8] c"Whether to enable preview mode. When preview mode is enabled, Ruff will use unstable rules, fixes, and formatting.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.458 = private unnamed_addr constant [813 x i8] c"A list of file patterns to exclude from formatting and linting.\0A\0AExclusions are based on globs, and can be either:\0A\0A- Single-path patterns, like `.mypy_cache` (to exclude any directory named `.mypy_cache` in the tree), `foo.py` (to exclude any file named `foo.py`), or `foo_*.py` (to exclude any file matching `foo_*.py` ). - Relative patterns, like `directory/foo.py` (to exclude that specific file) or `directory/*.py` (to exclude any Python files in `directory`). Note that these paths are relative to the project root (e.g., the directory containing your `pyproject.toml`).\0A\0AFor more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).\0A\0ANote that you'll typically want to use [`extend-exclude`](#extend-exclude) to modify the excluded paths.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.459 = private unnamed_addr constant [761 x i8] c"A list of file patterns to omit from formatting and linting, in addition to those specified by [`exclude`](#exclude).\0A\0AExclusions are based on globs, and can be either:\0A\0A- Single-path patterns, like `.mypy_cache` (to exclude any directory named `.mypy_cache` in the tree), `foo.py` (to exclude any file named `foo.py`), or `foo_*.py` (to exclude any file matching `foo_*.py` ). - Relative patterns, like `directory/foo.py` (to exclude that specific file) or `directory/*.py` (to exclude any Python files in `directory`). Note that these paths are relative to the project root (e.g., the directory containing your `pyproject.toml`).\0A\0AFor more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.460 = private unnamed_addr constant [363 x i8] c"A list of file patterns to include when linting, in addition to those specified by [`include`](#include).\0A\0AInclusion are based on globs, and should be single-path patterns, like `*.pyw`, to include any file with the `.pyw` extension.\0A\0AFor more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.461 = private unnamed_addr constant [586 x i8] c"Whether to enforce [`exclude`](#exclude) and [`extend-exclude`](#extend-exclude) patterns, even for paths that are passed to Ruff explicitly. Typically, Ruff will lint any paths passed in directly, even if they would typically be excluded. Setting `force-exclude = true` will cause Ruff to respect these exclusions unequivocally.\0A\0AThis is useful for [`pre-commit`](https://pre-commit.com/), which explicitly passes all changed files to the [`ruff-pre-commit`](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff-pre-commit) plugin, regardless of whether they're marked as excluded by Ruff's own settings.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.462 = private unnamed_addr constant [508 x i8] c"A list of file patterns to include when linting.\0A\0AInclusion are based on globs, and should be single-path patterns, like `*.pyw`, to include any file with the `.pyw` extension. `pyproject.toml` is included here not for configuration but because we lint whether e.g. the `[project]` matches the schema.\0A\0ANotebook files (`.ipynb` extension) are included by default on Ruff 0.6.0+.\0A\0AFor more information on the glob syntax, refer to the [`globset` documentation](https://docs.rs/globset/latest/globset/#syntax).", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.463 = private unnamed_addr constant [154 x i8] c"Whether to automatically exclude files that are ignored by `.ignore`, `.gitignore`, `.git/info/exclude`, and global `gitignore` files. Enabled by default.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.464 = private unnamed_addr constant [86 x i8] c"A list of builtins to treat as defined references, in addition to the system builtins.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.465 = private unnamed_addr constant [198 x i8] c"Mark the specified directories as namespace packages. For the purpose of module resolution, Ruff will treat those directories and all their subdirectories as if they contained an `__init__.py` file.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.466 = private unnamed_addr constant [1525 x i8] c"The minimum Python version to target, e.g., when considering automatic code upgrades, like rewriting type annotations. Ruff will not propose changes using features that are not available in the given version.\0A\0AFor example, to represent supporting Python >=3.10 or ==3.10 specify `target-version = \22py310\22`.\0A\0AIf you're already using a `pyproject.toml` file, we recommend `project.requires-python` instead, as it's based on Python packaging standards, and will be respected by other tools. For example, Ruff treats the following as identical to `target-version = \22py38\22`:\0A\0A```toml [project] requires-python = \22>=3.8\22 ```\0A\0AIf both are specified, `target-version` takes precedence over `requires-python`. See [_Inferring the Python version_](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/configuration/#inferring-the-python-version) for a complete description of how the `target-version` is determined when left unspecified.\0A\0ANote that a stub file can [sometimes make use of a typing feature](https://typing.python.org/en/latest/spec/distributing.html#syntax) before it is available at runtime, as long as the stub does not make use of new *syntax*. For example, a type checker will understand `int | str` in a stub as being a `Union` type annotation, even if the type checker is run using Python 3.9, despite the fact that the `|` operator can only be used to create union types at runtime on Python 3.10+. As such, Ruff will often recommend newer features in a stub file than it would for an equivalent runtime file with the same target version.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.467 = private unnamed_addr constant [752 x i8] c"A list of mappings from glob-style file pattern to Python version to use when checking the corresponding file(s).\0A\0AThis may be useful for overriding the global Python version settings in `target-version` or `requires-python` for a subset of files. For example, if you have a project with a minimum supported Python version of 3.9 but a subdirectory of developer scripts that want to use a newer feature like the `match` statement from Python 3.10, you can use `per-file-target-version` to specify `\22developer_scripts/*.py\22 = \22py310\22`.\0A\0AThis setting is used by the linter to enforce any enabled version-specific lint rules, as well as by the formatter for any version-specific formatting options, such as parenthesizing context managers on Python 3.10+.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.468 = private unnamed_addr constant [1227 x i8] c"The directories to consider when resolving first- vs. third-party imports.\0A\0AWhen omitted, the `src` directory will typically default to including both:\0A\0A1. The directory containing the nearest `pyproject.toml`, `ruff.toml`, or `.ruff.toml` file (the \22project root\22). 2. The `\22src\22` subdirectory of the project root.\0A\0AThese defaults ensure that Ruff supports both flat layouts and `src` layouts out-of-the-box. (If a configuration file is explicitly provided (e.g., via the `--config` command-line flag), the current working directory will be considered the project root.)\0A\0AAs an example, consider an alternative project structure, like:\0A\0A```text my_project \E2\94\9C\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 pyproject.toml \E2\94\94\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 lib \E2\94\94\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 my_package \E2\94\9C\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 __init__.py \E2\94\9C\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 foo.py \E2\94\94\E2\94\80\E2\94\80 bar.py ```\0A\0AIn this case, the `./lib` directory should be included in the `src` option (e.g., `src = [\22lib\22]`), such that when resolving imports, `my_package.foo` is considered first-party.\0A\0AThis field supports globs. For example, if you have a series of Python packages in a `python_modules` directory, `src = [\22python_modules/*\22]` would expand to incorporate all packages in that directory. User home directory and environment variables will also be expanded.", align 1
@anon.e792752b4454889dead1a66caae2c60f.469 = private unnamed_addr constant [772 x i8] c"The line length to use when enforcing long-lines violations (like `E501`) and at which `isort` and the formatter prefers to wrap lines.\0A\0AThe length is determined by the number of characters per line, except for lines containing East Asian characters or emojis. For these lines, the [unicode width](https://unicode.org/reports/tr11/) of each character is added up to determine the length.\0A\0AThe value must be greater than `0` and less than or equal to `320`.\0A\0ANote: While the formatter will attempt to format lines such that they remain within the `line-length`, it isn't a hard upper bound, and formatted lines may exceed the `line-length`.\0A\0ASee [`pycodestyle.max-line-length`](#lint_pycodestyle_max-line-length) to configure different lengths for `E501` and the formatter.", align 1
